\subsection{Repair Derived Addresses from Relocated Bases}

A compiler may keep an address into an object rather than the
object reference used to compute it. At a moving safepoint, the
interior address cannot be treated as a second ordinary root: it
does not necessarily identify an object boundary. Adopt a map
format with two kinds of entries, independently rewritable base
homes and derived homes with fixed byte offsets. Require every
derived home to name a listed, nonnull base, and prove that its
offset is within that object's layout. Assume the move preserves
the layout and the collector stops the thread until all fixups
are done. This simplified format leaves dynamic offsets to
ordinary compiler spills or to a richer map.

\begin{sudocode}[Update the roots, then rebuild derived values]
\ForAll{base homes $b$ in the frame map}
  \State $old\gets\Call{ReadHome}{b}$
  \If{$old$ is not null}
    \State $new\gets\Call{Forward}{old}$
    \State \Call{WriteHome}{$b,new$}
      \Comment{Update the rewritable root}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\ForAll{derived entries $(d,b,k)$ in the frame map}
  \State $base\gets\Call{ReadHome}{b}$ \Comment{Use the new base}
  \State \Call{WriteHome}{$d,base+k$}
\EndFor
\State resume only after both passes
\end{sudocode}

Let slot 2 hold base address 1000, and let a saved register hold
1012 for a field at offset 12. A forced collection moves the
object to 3400. The restored register must hold 3412 and read the
field's sentinel 27. Put the integer 1012 in a scalar slot as well;
that slot must remain 1012. Repeat with two derived homes at
offsets 4 and 12 to verify that both use one relocated base. Reject
a malformed map that deletes slot 2, even if an accidental copy of
the old base exists in another register.

\Cref{fig:visual-245} distinguishes two identical bit patterns before the collection: the map rebuilds the saved register but leaves the scalar alone.

